// Musculoskeletal — 450 questions
module.exports = [
  // --- Shoulder ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which rotator cuff muscle is MOST commonly involved in a full-thickness tear?', option_a:'Infraspinatus', option_b:'Supraspinatus', option_c:'Subscapularis', option_d:'Teres minor', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The supraspinatus is the most frequently torn rotator cuff muscle, likely due to its avascular zone near the insertion on the greater tubercle and its position under the coracoacromial arch during abduction.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A 55-year-old painter presents with right shoulder pain worsened by reaching overhead. Neer\'s test is positive. Hawkins-Kennedy test is positive. Empty can test shows pain with maintained strength (5/5). What is the MOST likely diagnosis?', option_a:'Full-thickness supraspinatus tear', option_b:'Subacromial impingement syndrome', option_c:'Acromioclavicular joint sprain', option_d:'Biceps tendon rupture', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Positive impingement signs (Neer, Hawkins-Kennedy) with a painful but strong empty can test indicates subacromial impingement without a full-thickness tear. Full tears typically show weakness and a positive drop arm test.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which test is MOST specific for thoracic outlet syndrome involving scalene compression?', option_a:'Phalen\'s test', option_b:'Adson\'s test', option_c:'Wright\'s test', option_d:'Roos test (EAST)', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Adson\'s test (ipsilateral head rotation + breath hold + extension) compresses the subclavian artery between the anterior and middle scalenes. A positive result (diminished radial pulse) indicates scalene-level TOS.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Roos test (EAST test) for thoracic outlet syndrome requires the patient to:', option_a:'Extend the neck while the examiner compresses the scalenes', option_b:'Hold both arms in 90° abduction and external rotation while opening and closing the fists for 3 minutes', option_c:'Horizontally adduct the arm while the examiner palpates the coracoid', option_d:'Perform the Phalen\'s position bilaterally for 60 seconds', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The Roos (EAST) test: patient holds arms in 90° ABD + ER (hands-up position) and repeatedly opens/closes fists for 3 minutes. Reproduction of symptoms (numbness, tingling, arm fatigue) indicates TOS. It is considered highly sensitive.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which structure is most commonly injured in a Grade III acromioclavicular (AC) joint separation?', option_a:'Glenohumeral capsule', option_b:'Coracoclavicular ligaments (conoid and trapezoid)', option_c:'Acromioclavicular ligament only', option_d:'Supraspinatus tendon', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'AC joint sprains: Grade I = AC ligament sprain only. Grade II = AC ligament rupture + CC ligament sprain. Grade III = complete AC + CC ligament rupture with superior clavicular displacement. The CC ligaments (conoid and trapezoid) maintain vertical stability.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient presents with weakness of shoulder external rotation and arm hanging in internal rotation following a traumatic delivery. Which nerve injury is MOST likely?', option_a:'Lower brachial plexus (Klumpke\'s palsy — C8-T1)', option_b:'Upper brachial plexus (Erb\'s palsy — C5-C6)', option_c:'Axillary nerve only', option_d:'Suprascapular nerve only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Erb\'s palsy (upper brachial plexus, C5-C6) from birth injury presents with the "waiter\'s tip" position: adducted, internally rotated shoulder, extended elbow, pronated forearm. The deltoid, biceps, brachialis, and external rotators are paralysed.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which muscle is responsible for the primary restraint against anterior glenohumeral instability?', option_a:'Deltoid', option_b:'Subscapularis', option_c:'Supraspinatus', option_d:'Long head of biceps', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The subscapularis (anterior rotator cuff) is the primary dynamic restraint against anterior instability. The inferior glenohumeral ligament (IGHL) is the primary static restraint at 90° ABD. Subscapularis insufficiency contributes to anterior instability.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Gerber lift-off test assesses the function of the:', option_a:'Infraspinatus', option_b:'Subscapularis', option_c:'Supraspinatus', option_d:'Teres minor', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The Gerber lift-off test: patient places their hand behind their back and lifts it away from the lumbar spine against resistance. Inability to lift the hand indicates subscapularis dysfunction. The belly press test is an alternative for subscapularis.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'During shoulder rehabilitation, open-chain exercises targeting the rotator cuff are performed at which range to minimize deltoid substitution and impingement risk?', option_a:'0–15° of abduction', option_b:'45–90° of abduction in the scapular plane', option_c:'90–120° of abduction', option_d:'Full overhead range only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Rotator cuff activation is optimized between 45–90° in the scapular plane (scaption). Below 30° the deltoid dominates; above 90° risk of impingement increases in the presence of pathology. Exercises in the scapular plane align the humerus with the glenoid optimally.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A 35-year-old volleyball player presents with anterior shoulder pain and an apprehension sign at 90° ABD + ER. Relocation test relieves the apprehension. What does this suggest?', option_a:'Posterior glenohumeral instability', option_b:'Anterior glenohumeral instability', option_c:'SLAP tear', option_d:'Subacromial impingement', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The apprehension test (positive with discomfort/apprehension at 90° ABD + ER) and positive relocation test (applying posterior force relieves apprehension) form the most reliable cluster for anterior glenohumeral instability.' },

  // --- Elbow/Forearm ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which nerve is most commonly compressed in cubital tunnel syndrome?', option_a:'Median nerve', option_b:'Ulnar nerve', option_c:'Radial nerve', option_d:'Anterior interosseous nerve', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Cubital tunnel syndrome involves compression of the ulnar nerve at the medial elbow. Symptoms include ring and small finger numbness/tingling (ulnar distribution) and intrinsic hand muscle weakness (interossei, hypothenar muscles). Tinel\'s sign at the medial elbow is positive.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient is unable to pinch the tip of the thumb to the tip of the index finger (instead uses pulp-to-pulp). Which nerve is most likely injured?', option_a:'Median nerve', option_b:'Anterior interosseous nerve (AIN)', option_c:'Radial nerve', option_d:'Ulnar nerve', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The AIN (branch of median nerve) innervates the FPL, pronator quadratus, and FDP to index/middle fingers. AIN palsy prevents tip-to-tip pinch because the FPL and index FDP are paralysed, resulting in the "OK sign" failure.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Lateral epicondylalgia (tennis elbow) is characterized pathologically as:', option_a:'An acute inflammatory process with neutrophil infiltration', option_b:'A degenerative tendinopathy with angiofibroblastic changes at the ECRB', option_c:'A stress fracture of the lateral epicondyle', option_d:'Entrapment of the radial nerve in the radial tunnel', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Histopathologically, lateral epicondylalgia is a degenerative tendinopathy (not tendinitis) characterized by angiofibroblastic changes, collagen disorganization, and neovascularization at the origin of the ECRB on the lateral epicondyle. Inflammatory cells are absent in chronic cases.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Valgus extension overload syndrome in baseball pitchers causes posterior olecranon osteophytes. Which phase of the throwing cycle creates maximum valgus stress at the elbow?', option_a:'Wind-up', option_b:'Late cocking / early acceleration', option_c:'Ball release', option_d:'Follow-through', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Maximum valgus stress at the medial elbow occurs during late cocking to early acceleration, when the elbow transitions from flexion-valgus position to extension. This creates tensile stress on the UCL medially and compressive stress on the lateral compartment.' },

  // --- Wrist/Hand ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Finkelstein\'s test is MOST diagnostic for:', option_a:'Carpal tunnel syndrome', option_b:'De Quervain\'s tenosynovitis', option_c:'Trigger finger', option_d:'Ulnar tunnel syndrome', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Finkelstein\'s test: the patient tucks their thumb into a fist, then the examiner passively deviates the wrist ulnarly. Reproduction of pain over the first dorsal compartment (APL and EPB tendons at the radial styloid) is positive for de Quervain\'s tenosynovitis.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The scaphoid bone receives its blood supply primarily:', option_a:'From the ulnar artery proximally', option_b:'Retrograde (distal to proximal) via branches of the radial artery', option_c:'From the anterior interosseous artery throughout', option_d:'From multiple equal proximal and distal supply sources', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The scaphoid receives its blood supply primarily through its distal pole via branches of the radial artery that enter distally. The proximal pole has poor vascularity. This retrograde pattern explains the high risk of avascular necrosis (AVN) following proximal scaphoid fractures.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) presents with ulnar deviation of the MCP joints and hyperextension of the PIP joints with flexion of the DIP joints. This deformity is called:', option_a:'Boutonnière deformity', option_b:'Swan-neck deformity', option_c:'Mallet finger', option_d:'Dupuytren\'s contracture', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Swan-neck deformity: PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion. It results from intrinsic muscle tightness or volar plate laxity, common in RA. Boutonnière is the opposite: PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension from central slip rupture.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The "prayer sign" (inability to fully oppose the palmar surfaces of both hands) is associated with:', option_a:'Carpal tunnel syndrome', option_b:'Diabetic cheiroarthropathy (limited joint mobility syndrome)', option_c:'Trigger finger', option_d:'De Quervain\'s tenosynovitis', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Diabetic cheiroarthropathy (limited joint mobility/stiff hand syndrome) is characterized by progressive painless stiffening of the finger joints due to glycosylation of collagen. The prayer sign (Table Top test) demonstrates inability to fully flatten the hand against a table or appose both palms.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Dupuytren\'s contracture most commonly involves which digit?', option_a:'Index and middle fingers', option_b:'Ring and small fingers', option_c:'Thumb only', option_d:'Middle finger only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Dupuytren\'s disease involves fibroproliferative thickening of the palmar fascia leading to MCP and PIP flexion contractures, most commonly affecting the ring and small fingers. It is associated with northern European ancestry, diabetes, alcohol use, and anticonvulsants.' },

  // --- Spine ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Spurling test (cervical foraminal compression test) is performed by:', option_a:'Applying upward traction to the cervical spine', option_b:'Laterally flexing and rotating the head toward the symptomatic side and applying downward compression', option_c:'Passive flexion of the cervical spine only', option_d:'Bilateral compression of the cervical spine in neutral', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Spurling\'s test: lateral flexion and rotation toward the symptomatic side + axial compression. This narrows the ipsilateral foramen and reproduces radicular symptoms (pain, paresthesia into the arm). It has high specificity (~93%) but lower sensitivity (~50%) for cervical radiculopathy.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Upper cervical instability testing (Sharp-Purser test) screens for instability at which joint?', option_a:'C3-C4 facet joint', option_b:'C0-C1 (atlanto-occipital) joint', option_c:'C1-C2 (atlantoaxial) joint', option_d:'C4-C5 disc', correct_option_index:2, rationale:'The Sharp-Purser test assesses atlantoaxial (C1-C2) instability. The examiner stabilizes the axis (C2) and applies a posterior-directed force to the atlas. A "clunk" or symptom reduction indicates excessive atlantodental interval. It screens for transverse ligament insufficiency (e.g., Down syndrome, RA, trauma).' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Modified-Modified Schober test measures:', option_a:'Hip flexor length', option_b:'Lumbar flexion excursion', option_c:'Thoracic kyphosis angle', option_d:'Sacroiliac joint mobility', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The Modified-Modified Schober test measures lumbar spine flexion mobility. Marks are placed 5 cm below and 10 cm above the PSIS. The patient flexes maximally; a normal increase is >5 cm (from 15 cm to >20 cm). Less than 5 cm increase indicates restricted lumbar flexion, as seen in ankylosing spondylitis.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A positive prone instability test (PIT) is characterized by:', option_a:'Increased pain in prone with posterior-anterior pressure, relieved when the patient actively lifts legs off the table', option_b:'Reproduction of sciatic symptoms with prone knee bend', option_c:'Pain provocation with passive extension in prone', option_d:'Inability to maintain the prone position due to pain', correct_option_index:0, rationale:'The Prone Instability Test: PA pressure applied to the lumbar spine in prone reproduces pain. The patient then lifts their feet off the table (activating lumbar stabilizers), and the PA pressure is re-applied. Pain reduction with active muscle contraction = positive PIT, suggesting lumbar segmental instability.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which lumbar vertebral level is at the level of the iliac crests (Tuffier\'s line)?', option_a:'L2-L3', option_b:'L4-L5', option_c:'L3-L4', option_d:'L5-S1', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The iliac crests (Tuffier\'s line) correspond approximately to the L4-L5 interspace. This landmark is used clinically to estimate vertebral levels for spinal injection, lumbar puncture, and manual therapy interventions.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'In the McKenzie Method, which classification responds to repeated end-range flexion exercises?', option_a:'Extension derangement', option_b:'Posterior derangement', option_c:'Anterior derangement with flexion preference', option_d:'Dysfunction syndrome', correct_option_index:2, rationale:'Some patients have a flexion directional preference where repeated lumbar flexion centralizes or abolishes their symptoms. This indicates an anterior derangement. These patients benefit from a flexion principle approach (knee-to-chest, lumbar flexion exercises). The majority of derangements respond to extension.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The clinical prediction rule (CPR) for lumbar spinal manipulation (Flynn et al.) includes all of the following EXCEPT:', option_a:'Duration of symptoms <16 days', option_b:'No symptoms distal to the knee', option_c:'Hypomobility on lumbar spring testing', option_d:'Positive straight leg raise test', correct_option_index:3, rationale:'Flynn\'s CPR for lumbar manipulation (5 criteria): (1) symptom duration <16 days, (2) no symptoms distal to the knee, (3) FABQ work subscale <19, (4) at least one hypomobile lumbar segment, (5) hip IR >35° in at least one hip. A positive SLR is NOT a criterion and may be a contraindication.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Cauda equina syndrome is a RED FLAG characterized by:', option_a:'Bilateral buttock pain only', option_b:'Bladder/bowel dysfunction, saddle anaesthesia, and bilateral lower extremity weakness', option_c:'Unilateral sciatica with positive SLR', option_d:'Severe localized low back pain without neurological signs', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Cauda equina syndrome is a surgical emergency caused by compression of the cauda equina nerve roots. Cardinal features: urinary retention or incontinence, faecal incontinence, saddle (perineal) anaesthesia, and bilateral lower extremity weakness/numbness. Immediate medical referral/imaging is mandatory.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which exercise approach has the MOST evidence for chronic non-specific low back pain?', option_a:'Motor control/stabilization exercises are significantly superior to all other approaches', option_b:'Multiple exercise types (aerobic, strengthening, Pilates, yoga) have similar efficacy; the best approach is the one the patient will adhere to', option_c:'Bed rest followed by gradual mobilization', option_d:'Extension-only exercises (McKenzie) are superior for all patients', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Systematic reviews for chronic NLBP show that many types of exercise (motor control, aerobic, strengthening, Pilates, yoga) have similar effectiveness. Adherence and patient preference are the strongest predictors of outcome. A personalized approach based on patient preferences and capabilities is recommended by all major guidelines.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Thoracic outlet syndrome (TOS) can involve compression of the brachial plexus between which two structures in the costoclavicular space?', option_a:'Pectoralis major and coracoid process', option_b:'Clavicle and first rib', option_c:'Anterior and middle scalenes', option_d:'Coracoacromial ligament and greater tubercle', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Costoclavicular TOS involves compression of the brachial plexus and subclavian vessels between the clavicle (superiorly) and the first rib (inferiorly). The costoclavicular (military brace) test provokes this compression. Scalene TOS occurs between the scalenes; pectoralis minor TOS occurs at the pectoralis minor.' },

  // --- Hip ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which of the following is the MOST sensitive test for femoroacetabular impingement (FAI)?', option_a:'FABER test', option_b:'FADDIR test (flexion, adduction, internal rotation)', option_c:'Trendelenburg test', option_d:'Thomas test', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The FADDIR test (combined hip flexion to 90°, adduction, and internal rotation) is the most sensitive clinical test for FAI, reproducing the impingement of the femoral neck against the acetabular rim. Sensitivity is ~78–95% but specificity is low, so it is best used as a screening tool.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A 17-year-old obese male presents with an antalgic gait, external rotation of the right hip, and decreased internal rotation. X-ray shows a "ice cream falling off the cone" appearance at the right hip. What is the MOST likely diagnosis?', option_a:'Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease', option_b:'Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE)', option_c:'Developmental dysplasia of the hip', option_d:'Avascular necrosis', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'SCFE occurs in overweight adolescents (10–16 years) with disruption of the proximal femoral growth plate. The femoral head slips posteriorly and inferiorly relative to the neck. The "ice cream off the cone" X-ray appearance and obligatory external rotation with hip flexion are classic. Urgent orthopedic referral is required.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'What is the hip abductor moment arm and why is it important?', option_a:'The angle of the femoral neck — smaller angles improve leverage', option_b:'The perpendicular distance from the hip joint axis to the line of action of the abductor muscles — greater moment arms reduce joint reaction forces', option_c:'The length of the iliotibial band from ASIS to tibia', option_d:'The distance between the ASIS and greater trochanter only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The hip abductor moment arm is the perpendicular distance from the hip joint center to the line of action of the abductors (gluteus medius). Greater moment arm = more torque per unit force = less muscle force needed = lower joint reaction force. Valgus osteotomy or lateral femoral offset in THA increases the moment arm.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which hip muscle is the MOST important for controlling contralateral pelvic drop during single-limb stance?', option_a:'Gluteus maximus', option_b:'Gluteus medius', option_c:'Iliopsoas', option_d:'Tensor fascia latae alone', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Gluteus medius on the weight-bearing limb prevents contralateral pelvic drop (Trendelenburg sign) by generating an adductor moment to stabilize the pelvis during single-limb support. Weakness causes the pelvis to drop toward the swing side (positive Trendelenburg) or trunk lurch toward the stance side (compensated Trendelenburg).' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Following posterior total hip arthroplasty, which combination of movements must be AVOIDED to prevent posterior dislocation?', option_a:'Hip extension, abduction, external rotation', option_b:'Hip flexion >90°, adduction across midline, internal rotation', option_c:'Hip flexion >90°, abduction, external rotation', option_d:'Hip flexion >60°, adduction, internal rotation', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Posterior approach THA precautions: avoid hip flexion >90° (or sometimes >60° initially), adduction past midline, and internal rotation. These movements tighten the posterior structures and can lever the femoral head over the posterior capsule, causing posterior dislocation.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Piriformis syndrome is characterized by buttock pain with radiation that is TYPICALLY worsened by:', option_a:'Lying supine with hip flexed and externally rotated', option_b:'Prolonged sitting, hip internal rotation, or adduction (FAIR position)', option_c:'Hip extension exercises only', option_d:'Lumbar extension', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Piriformis syndrome involves irritation of the sciatic nerve by the piriformis muscle. Symptoms are provoked by prolonged sitting, hip flexion + adduction + internal rotation (FAIR position), and climbing stairs. The FAIR test (passive stretch of piriformis) and Pace test (resisted hip abduction/ER) are used clinically.' },

  // --- Knee ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The pivot shift test assesses for:', option_a:'Posterior cruciate ligament insufficiency', option_b:'ACL insufficiency causing anterolateral rotatory instability', option_c:'Lateral meniscus tear', option_d:'Lateral collateral ligament sprain', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The pivot shift test assesses anterolateral rotatory instability (ALRI) from ACL insufficiency. With the knee near extension, the iliotibial band subluxes the tibia anteriorly; with applied valgus and internal rotation, the tibia reduces as the knee flexes to ~30°. This "clunk" or "glide" is a positive pivot shift.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which test is MOST sensitive for posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) tear?', option_a:'Lachman test', option_b:'Posterior sag sign (posterior drawer at 90° flexion)', option_c:'Valgus stress test', option_d:'McMurray test', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'PCL tears present with posterior tibial sag when the knee is flexed to 90° (posterior sag sign/Goddfrey\'s sign). The posterior drawer test (posterior tibial translation at 90° flexion) is also specific. These are differentiated from ACL testing which assesses anterior translation.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient with medial knee pain, tenderness 5 cm below the medial joint line at the pes anserine insertion, and morning stiffness. No joint effusion. Most likely diagnosis?', option_a:'Medial meniscus tear', option_b:'Pes anserine bursitis', option_c:'Medial collateral ligament sprain', option_d:'Medial compartment osteoarthritis', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Pes anserine bursitis presents with tenderness at the insertion of the pes anserine tendons (sartorius, gracilis, semitendinosus) on the anteromedial tibia, approximately 4–6 cm below the medial joint line. Common in middle-aged, overweight women with knee OA. No joint effusion distinguishes it from intra-articular pathology.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which test for meniscal pathology involves applying varus/valgus stress with knee rotation?', option_a:'Thessaly test', option_b:'McMurray test', option_c:'Apley\'s test', option_d:'Bounce home test', correct_option_index:2, rationale:'Apley\'s test: patient lies prone, knee flexed 90°. Examiner applies axial compression + rotation (grinding = meniscal) vs. distraction + rotation (ligamentous). The McMurray test is performed supine with combined flexion-rotation. The Thessaly test involves single-leg standing at 20° flexion with rotation.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A 14-year-old girl presents with anterior knee pain reproducible by compression of the patella (Clarke\'s sign), lateral patellar tilt, and pain on stair descent. There is no effusion. MOST likely diagnosis?', option_a:'Hoffa\'s fat pad impingement', option_b:'Patellofemoral pain syndrome', option_c:'Patellar tendinopathy', option_d:'Tibial plateau fracture', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Patellofemoral pain syndrome (PFPS) is common in adolescent girls. Features: anterior knee pain with stairs/squatting/prolonged sitting, positive Clarke\'s test (patellar compression), lateral patellar tilt, no joint effusion. It is the most common knee diagnosis in outpatient PT settings.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The "screw-home mechanism" of the knee occurs during terminal extension. During open-chain (non-weight-bearing) knee extension, the tibia:', option_a:'Internally rotates', option_b:'Externally rotates', option_c:'Translates anteriorly without rotation', option_d:'Adducts', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'During open-chain knee extension, the tibia externally rotates (screw-home mechanism) in the last 5–10° of extension, locking the knee in a close-packed position. In closed chain (weight-bearing), the femur internally rotates on the fixed tibia to produce the same locking effect.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which Q-angle measurement is associated with increased patellofemoral pain risk?', option_a:'>5° in males or >10° in females', option_b:'>15° in males or >20° in females', option_c:'<5° in both sexes', option_d:'Any angle >10° regardless of sex', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The Q-angle (from ASIS through patella center to tibial tuberosity) average values: males ~10–15°, females ~15–20°. Q-angles >15° in males or >20° in females are associated with increased lateral patellar tracking forces and patellofemoral pain. Females have greater Q-angles due to wider pelvis relative to femur length.' },

  // --- Ankle/Foot ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which ligament is MOST commonly sprained in a lateral ankle sprain?', option_a:'Calcaneofibular ligament (CFL)', option_b:'Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)', option_c:'Posterior talofibular ligament (PTFL)', option_d:'Deltoid ligament', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'In lateral ankle sprains (inversion mechanism), the ATFL is the most commonly injured ligament (isolated in Grade I; combined with CFL in Grade II/III). The PTFL is rarely injured and only in severe sprains. The deltoid is a medial ligament injured with eversion.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Thompson test (calf squeeze test) is used to assess:', option_a:'Ankle ligament stability', option_b:'Achilles tendon rupture', option_c:'Peroneal tendon subluxation', option_d:'Tibialis posterior tendon insufficiency', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The Thompson (calf squeeze) test: with the patient prone, the examiner squeezes the calf. Absence of plantar flexion is a positive test indicating Achilles tendon rupture. Intact plantarflexion rules out complete tendon rupture. Simple clinical test with high sensitivity (>95%) and specificity.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Plantar fasciitis most commonly presents with pain:', option_a:'At the insertion of the plantar fascia on the calcaneus, worst with the first steps after rest', option_b:'At the mid-arch, worsening throughout the day', option_c:'In the plantar toes, exacerbated by prolonged standing', option_d:'At the lateral heel with lateral ankle inversion', correct_option_index:0, rationale:'Plantar fasciitis classically causes inferior heel pain at the calcaneal insertion of the plantar fascia, worst with the first steps in the morning or after prolonged rest (post-static dyskinesia). Pain may improve with walking then worsen again after extended activity.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Tarsal tunnel syndrome involves compression of which nerve?', option_a:'Sural nerve', option_b:'Posterior tibial nerve', option_c:'Deep peroneal nerve', option_d:'Superficial peroneal nerve', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Tarsal tunnel syndrome is compression of the posterior tibial nerve (or its branches: medial plantar, lateral plantar, medial calcaneal nerves) in the tarsal tunnel under the flexor retinaculum, posterior to the medial malleolus. Symptoms: burning, tingling on the plantar foot; Tinel\'s sign at the medial ankle is positive.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which structure is assessed by the Kleiger (external rotation stress) test at the ankle?', option_a:'ATFL', option_b:'Distal tibiofibular syndesmosis and deltoid ligament', option_c:'Spring ligament', option_d:'Plantar fascia', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The Kleiger test: with the knee flexed to 90°, the examiner externally rotates the foot. Medial ankle pain indicates deltoid ligament involvement; lateral pain (at the fibula) indicates syndesmotic injury. A "high ankle sprain" (syndesmotic injury) is more disabling than a lateral ankle sprain and takes longer to recover.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Morton\'s neuroma most commonly occurs between which metatarsal heads?', option_a:'1st and 2nd', option_b:'3rd and 4th', option_c:'2nd and 3rd', option_d:'4th and 5th', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Morton\'s neuroma (perineural fibrosis of the plantar digital nerve) most commonly occurs in the third web space (between the 3rd and 4th metatarsal heads), followed by the second web space. Mulder\'s click and web space compression are diagnostic. Symptoms: burning, shooting pain in the affected toes.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Balance training after ankle sprains reduces re-injury risk by improving:', option_a:'Ligament tensile strength directly', option_b:'Proprioceptive function and peroneal muscle reaction time', option_c:'Bone mineral density at the distal fibula', option_d:'Inflammatory response in the ATFL', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Ankle sprains damage proprioceptive mechanoreceptors in the joint capsule and ligaments. Balance/proprioceptive training on unstable surfaces (wobble boards, BOSU) improves proprioceptive feedback, enhances peroneal muscle reaction time, and reduces re-injury rates by up to 50% in Level I RCTs.' },

  // --- Biomechanics/Gait ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'During the loading response of gait, which muscle group is eccentrically active to control knee flexion?', option_a:'Hamstrings', option_b:'Quadriceps', option_c:'Plantarflexors', option_d:'Hip abductors', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'During loading response (0–10% of gait cycle), the knee flexes approximately 15° to absorb shock. The quadriceps eccentrically control this flexion by decelerating the knee. This is the most demanding phase for the quadriceps in gait, and quadriceps weakness leads to knee buckling or compensatory forward trunk lean.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Stride length is defined as:', option_a:'The distance from heel strike of one foot to heel strike of the same foot', option_b:'The distance from heel strike of one foot to heel strike of the OPPOSITE foot', option_c:'The width between the two feet during double limb support', option_d:'The time to complete one full gait cycle', correct_option_index:0, rationale:'Stride length = the distance from heel strike (initial contact) of one foot to the next heel strike of the SAME foot (one complete gait cycle). Step length = heel strike of one foot to heel strike of the OTHER foot. Normal adult stride length: ~1.35–1.55 m.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient walks with the trunk leaning toward the right during right stance phase. This is MOST likely due to:', option_a:'Right hip flexor weakness', option_b:'Right hip abductor weakness (compensated Trendelenburg)', option_c:'Left hip abductor weakness', option_d:'Right knee extensor weakness', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'In compensated Trendelenburg gait, the patient lurches the trunk toward the weak stance side (right, in this case) to shift the center of mass over the hip, reducing the demand on the right hip abductors. This contrasts with uncompensated Trendelenburg where the pelvis drops to the opposite side.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which gait deviation is MOST characteristic of a bilateral peroneal nerve palsy?', option_a:'Toe walking', option_b:'High steppage gait (bilateral foot drop)', option_c:'Trendelenburg gait', option_d:'Scissor gait', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Peroneal nerve palsy causes foot drop (weakness of ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension). To clear the foot during swing phase, the patient exaggerates hip flexion and knee flexion, creating a steppage (high-stepping) gait. Bilateral peroneal palsy produces bilateral high steppage gait.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Running produces peak ground reaction forces of approximately:', option_a:'0.5–1 × body weight', option_b:'2–3 × body weight', option_c:'5–8 × body weight', option_d:'Equal to body weight', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Running produces peak vertical GRFs of approximately 2–3 × body weight (compared to ~1.2 × BW during walking). These forces must be absorbed by the musculoskeletal system. Overuse injuries in runners are largely attributable to accumulated loading over training volume.' },

  // --- Osteoporosis/Bone ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A DEXA scan shows a T-score of -2.6. This indicates:', option_a:'Normal bone density', option_b:'Osteopenia', option_c:'Osteoporosis', option_d:'Severe osteoporosis', correct_option_index:2, rationale:'WHO T-score criteria: ≥-1.0 = normal; -1.0 to -2.4 = osteopenia; ≤-2.5 = osteoporosis; ≤-2.5 with fragility fracture = severe osteoporosis. A T-score of -2.6 meets the osteoporosis threshold. T-score represents SD below the mean peak bone mass of a healthy 30-year-old adult.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which type of exercise produces the GREATEST osteogenic stimulus to prevent osteoporosis?', option_a:'Aquatic exercise and swimming', option_b:'High-impact, high-magnitude progressive resistance training and impact loading', option_c:'Seated resistance machines only', option_d:'Stretching and yoga', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Bone remodeling is stimulated by high-magnitude, high-rate loading. Progressive resistance training combined with high-impact weight-bearing activities (jumping, jogging, tennis) provides the greatest osteogenic stimulus. Aquatic exercise lacks the gravitational loading needed; the Wolff\'s Law principle applies — bone adapts to the loads placed upon it.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A hip fracture in an elderly patient results from a fall. Which fracture type carries the HIGHEST mortality risk at 1 year?', option_a:'Femoral neck (intracapsular) fracture', option_b:'Intertrochanteric fracture', option_c:'Subtrochanteric fracture', option_d:'All have equal mortality', correct_option_index:0, rationale:'Femoral neck (intracapsular) fractures have the highest mortality risk (15–30% at 1 year) due to disruption of the blood supply to the femoral head (risk of AVN), requirement for hemiarthroplasty in displaced fractures, and older patient population affected. Intertrochanteric fractures also carry high mortality but slightly less than neck fractures.' },

  // --- Soft Tissue/Tendon ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Continuum Model of tendon pathology by Cook and Purdam classifies tendinopathy into three stages. The stage characterized by reversible cellular changes with no structural compromise is:', option_a:'Degenerative tendinopathy', option_b:'Reactive tendinopathy', option_c:'Tendon disrepair', option_d:'Tendon rupture', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Cook and Purdam\'s Continuum Model: (1) Reactive tendinopathy — acute, reversible, no structural damage; (2) Tendon disrepair — failed healing attempt with matrix disruption; (3) Degenerative tendinopathy — irreversible cell death and collagen disorganization. Treatment differs by stage.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The VISA-A questionnaire is used to measure severity of:', option_a:'Knee patellofemoral pain', option_b:'Achilles tendinopathy', option_c:'Rotator cuff pathology', option_d:'Lateral epicondylalgia', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The VISA-A (Victorian Institute of Sport Assessment — Achilles) is a validated patient-reported outcome measuring symptoms, simple tests of function, and ability to participate in sport in patients with Achilles tendinopathy. VISA-P is for patellar tendinopathy; VISA-S for shoulder.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which component of the PEACE & LOVE framework for soft tissue injury management represents a newer addition replacing the PRICE principle?', option_a:'Elevation', option_b:'Optimism, Load management, and vascularisation (active recovery)', option_c:'Compression and ice', option_d:'Protection only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'PEACE & LOVE (2019 framework): PEACE = Protect, Elevate, Avoid anti-inflammatories, Compress, Educate; LOVE = Load, Optimism, Vascularization, Exercise. Key additions: avoiding anti-inflammatories (they may impair healing), early loading, and addressing psychosocial factors (optimism). This replaces RICE/PRICE.' },

  // --- Fractures/Post-surgical ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient is 6 weeks post-ORIF fixation of a distal radius fracture. The surgeon has cleared full weight-bearing and ROM. Which splinting position is MOST appropriate for nighttime to prevent contractures?', option_a:'Full wrist and finger flexion', option_b:'Wrist neutral or slight extension, fingers in composite extension (anti-deformity position)', option_c:'Wrist in maximum extension with fingers flexed', option_d:'Thumb in adduction and opposition', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'After distal radius ORIF, the anti-deformity splint positions the wrist in neutral to slight extension with fingers in full composite extension (MPs flexed, IPs extended). This prevents intrinsic-minus contracture and maintains web space. This is contrasted with the burn splint position where MCPs are flexed.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Following ACL reconstruction with patellar tendon autograft, which exercise is most appropriate at 4 weeks post-op?', option_a:'Full squat to parallel depth with resistance', option_b:'Stationary cycling with low resistance and heel strikes, quad sets, and gait training', option_c:'Running program initiation', option_d:'Return to sport', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'At 4 weeks post ACL reconstruction (graft fixation, not yet mature), focus is on: controlling effusion, full ROM (especially extension), quadriceps activation (quad sets, SLR), gentle closed-chain exercises (mini-squats 0–45°), and stationary cycling. Running is typically initiated at 3–4 months; return to sport at 9–12 months.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which criteria must be met before return to sport after ACL reconstruction?', option_a:'Time from surgery alone (9 months is sufficient)', option_b:'Limb symmetry index (LSI) ≥90% for strength and hop tests, plus psychological readiness', option_c:'Absence of pain only', option_d:'Completion of formal physiotherapy regardless of functional outcome', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Current evidence-based ACL return-to-sport criteria require: LSI ≥90% for quadriceps/hamstring strength (isokinetic dynamometry), ≥90% on hop test battery (single-hop, triple-hop, cross-over hop), and psychological readiness (ACL-RSI or K-SES ≥65%). Time alone is insufficient; functional criteria must be met.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient 12 weeks post total knee arthroplasty (TKA) should have achieved which ROM goal for functional activities?', option_a:'30° of flexion (adequate for flat walking)', option_b:'≥90° of flexion for stair climbing; ≥110° for ADLs like getting up from low chairs', option_c:'Full 140° flexion by 12 weeks', option_d:'ROM is irrelevant; only strength matters at 12 weeks', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'TKA ROM benchmarks: 90° is required for stair climbing; 110–115° for comfortable ADLs (kneeling, low chairs, car transfers); 120–130° is excellent functional range. Most patients reach 90° by 4–6 weeks post-TKA. At 12 weeks, targets are ≥110–120°. Full 140° is a late (6–12 month) goal, if achievable.' },

  // --- Muscle Physiology ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Type I muscle fibers are characterized by:', option_a:'Fast twitch, high glycolytic, fatigues quickly', option_b:'Slow twitch, high oxidative, fatigue resistant, smaller motor units', option_c:'Fast twitch, mixed oxidative-glycolytic, intermediate fatigue resistance', option_d:'Large diameter, high force production, low endurance', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Type I (slow-twitch, oxidative) fibers: small, fatigue-resistant, high mitochondrial density, rely on aerobic metabolism, recruited first in voluntary contraction. They are suited for postural and endurance tasks. Type IIa (fast-oxidative-glycolytic) and IIx/b (fast-glycolytic) fibers are faster, larger, and more fatigable.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The SAID principle (Specific Adaptations to Imposed Demands) means that training adaptations are:', option_a:'Generic — any exercise improves all types of fitness', option_b:'Specific to the type, speed, and range of motion of training', option_c:'Primarily dependent on training volume, not type', option_d:'Only applicable to athletes, not rehabilitation patients', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'SAID principle: the body adapts specifically to the demands placed upon it. Strength training produces strength adaptations; endurance training produces cardiovascular adaptations. Training at specific speeds, ROM, and positions optimizes adaptation at those parameters. This is critical in rehabilitation when designing exercises that transfer to functional tasks.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Henneman Size Principle of motor unit recruitment states that:', option_a:'Larger, high-threshold motor units are recruited before smaller ones during voluntary contraction', option_b:'Smaller, low-threshold (Type I) motor units are recruited first; larger motor units are added as force demand increases', option_c:'Motor units are recruited randomly during voluntary contraction', option_d:'All motor units are recruited simultaneously for maximum force', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Henneman\'s Size Principle: motor units are recruited in order of size, from smallest (low threshold, Type I, slow-twitch) to largest (high threshold, Type II, fast-twitch) as force requirements increase. This allows precise, graded force control during low-intensity tasks while recruiting larger units for high-force demands.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which of the following BEST describes delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS)?', option_a:'Acute lactic acid buildup during exercise', option_b:'Microstructural damage and inflammatory response peaking 24–72 hours after unaccustomed eccentric exercise', option_c:'Acute cramping during exercise due to electrolyte imbalance', option_d:'Tendon injury from excessive loading', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'DOMS results from microstructural damage (Z-disc disruption, sarcomere disruption) and subsequent inflammatory response following unaccustomed eccentric loading. It peaks at 24–72 hours post-exercise and resolves within 5–7 days. Lactic acid clears within an hour of exercise cessation and is NOT the cause of DOMS.' },

  // --- Manual Therapy ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Maitland mobilization Grade IV involves:', option_a:'Large amplitude oscillations near the beginning of range', option_b:'Small amplitude oscillations at or near end range', option_c:'High velocity, low amplitude thrust at end range', option_d:'Large amplitude oscillations through the full range', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Maitland Grades: I = small amplitude at start of range; II = large amplitude not reaching end range; III = large amplitude into resistance/end range; IV = small amplitude at end range (stiffness); V = HVLA manipulation. Grades III-IV primarily address stiffness; I-II primarily address pain.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Joint mobilization is generally CONTRAINDICATED in which situation?', option_a:'Subacute muscle strain', option_b:'Malignancy (primary or metastatic) involving the region to be treated', option_c:'Joint stiffness without inflammation', option_d:'Mild to moderate pain at end range', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Absolute contraindications to joint mobilization include: malignancy in the treatment region, acute fracture, joint infection (septic arthritis), ligamentous rupture (unstable joint), spinal cord compression, and inflammatory disease in the acute phase. Malignant bone lesions are particularly important to screen for before spinal manipulation.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The concave-convex rule states that when a concave surface moves on a fixed convex surface, the roll and slide occur in:', option_a:'Opposite directions', option_b:'The same direction', option_c:'A random direction depending on the joint angle', option_d:'No sliding component occurs', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Concave-convex rule: (1) Convex on concave = roll and slide in OPPOSITE directions (to maintain joint contact). (2) Concave on convex = roll and slide in the SAME direction. This guides accessory mobilization direction — e.g., posterior glide for gaining shoulder flexion (convex humeral head on concave glenoid requires opposite direction glide).' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Cervical artery dysfunction (CAD) screening before cervical manipulation is recommended to identify risk of:', option_a:'Cervical disc herniation', option_b:'Vertebrobasilar artery stroke or dissection', option_c:'Cervical facet joint arthropathy', option_d:'Thoracic outlet syndrome', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Vertebrobasilar insufficiency screening (pre-manipulative testing) before high-velocity cervical manipulation is performed to identify risk of vertebral or internal carotid artery dissection. Current evidence suggests the 5D\'s and 3N\'s mnemonic (dizziness, drop attacks, diplopia, dysphagia, dysarthria, nausea, nystagmus, numbness of face) identifies high-risk patients.' },

  // --- Electrophysical Agents ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Therapeutic ultrasound at 3 MHz is used primarily for:', option_a:'Deep tissue heating of muscles >3 cm depth', option_b:'Superficial tissue heating (within 1–2 cm depth)', option_c:'Nerve stimulation at the deep level', option_d:'Breaking up calcified deposits only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Frequency determines depth penetration of therapeutic ultrasound: 1 MHz penetrates deeper (3–5 cm), used for deeper muscles and joints. 3 MHz penetrates shallower (1–2 cm) and is used for superficial soft tissue (tendons, ligaments, skin). Continuous US provides thermal effects; pulsed US provides non-thermal effects.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'TENS (transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation) for pain relief via the gate control theory uses:', option_a:'Low frequency, high intensity stimulation to produce muscle contractions', option_b:'High frequency, low intensity stimulation to activate large-diameter A-beta fibers', option_c:'Medium frequency alternating current to penetrate deeply', option_d:'DC current to drive medication into tissue', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Conventional TENS (gate control mechanism): high frequency (80–150 Hz), low intensity (comfortable tingling), produces activation of A-beta (large diameter, myelinated) sensory fibers that inhibit pain transmission at the dorsal horn. Acupuncture-like TENS uses low frequency (1–4 Hz) high intensity to trigger endorphin release.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Iontophoresis uses direct electrical current to:', option_a:'Heat superficial tissue', option_b:'Drive charged medication molecules transdermally into tissue', option_c:'Stimulate muscle contractions', option_d:'Break up adhesions in tendons', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Iontophoresis uses a direct current to drive ionically charged drug molecules through the skin (transdermal delivery) without injection. Positive ions (e.g., dexamethasone) are driven from the positive electrode; negative ions from the negative electrode. Common uses: anti-inflammatory medication for tendinopathy, hyperhidrosis.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Cold therapy (cryotherapy) is MOST effective for:', option_a:'Increasing tissue extensibility before stretching', option_b:'Reducing acute post-traumatic edema and pain through vasoconstriction and nerve conduction velocity reduction', option_c:'Increasing blood flow and metabolic rate in healing tissue', option_d:'Treating chronic inflammatory conditions primarily', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Cryotherapy in the acute phase reduces: metabolic rate (slowing secondary hypoxic injury), nerve conduction velocity (analgesia), and vascular permeability (vasoconstriction reduces edema). Ice/cold is most effective in the first 24–48 hours post-injury. Heat (thermotherapy) is preferred for subacute/chronic conditions to increase extensibility.' },

  // --- Evidence-based Practice MSK ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'According to the Ottawa Ankle Rules, an ankle X-ray is indicated when:', option_a:'Any mechanism of injury causes ankle pain', option_b:'There is bony tenderness at the posterior edge/tip of either malleolus OR inability to weight-bear 4 steps immediately and in the ED', option_c:'Swelling is present regardless of weight-bearing ability', option_d:'The patient reports pain >7/10', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Ottawa Ankle Rules: X-ray indicated if (1) bony tenderness at the posterior 6 cm or tip of the lateral malleolus, OR posterior 6 cm or tip of the medial malleolus; OR (2) inability to weight-bear 4 steps immediately after injury and in the ED. Sensitivity approaches 100% for fracture detection, significantly reducing unnecessary imaging.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Ottawa Knee Rules recommend X-ray when any of the following are present EXCEPT:', option_a:'Age ≥55 years', option_b:'Isolated patellar tenderness', option_c:'Inability to flex knee to 90°', option_d:'Presence of a joint effusion only', correct_option_index:3, rationale:'Ottawa Knee Rules: X-ray indicated for: age ≥55; isolated patellar tenderness (no other bony tenderness); fibular head tenderness; inability to flex knee to 90°; inability to weight-bear immediately and in ED. A joint effusion alone is NOT an OKR criterion; it requires clinical judgment but does not mandate X-ray.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which intervention has the STRONGEST evidence for lateral epicondylalgia?', option_a:'Corticosteroid injection for long-term outcomes', option_b:'Eccentric and progressive exercise programs combined with manual therapy', option_c:'Surgery within 3 months of onset', option_d:'Complete rest and immobilization', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'For lateral epicondylalgia, high-quality RCTs show: corticosteroid injections provide short-term benefit but worse long-term outcomes than exercise or wait-and-see. Exercise-based approaches (progressive loading/eccentric training) combined with manual therapy have the best evidence for durable improvement at 6–12 months.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Minimal Detectable Change (MDC) for shoulder active flexion range of motion measured with a goniometer is approximately:', option_a:'2°', option_b:'12°', option_c:'25°', option_d:'45°', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The MDC for shoulder ROM using a standard goniometer is approximately 7–12° for most motions. This means a change of at least 10–12° is needed to exceed measurement error and represent a real change in ROM. Clinical significance requires exceeding the MDC first, then the MCID for the patient population.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The number needed to treat (NNT) for an intervention is 8. This means:', option_a:'8 patients must be excluded for every 1 treated', option_b:'You need to treat 8 patients to achieve 1 additional positive outcome compared to control', option_c:'The treatment works in 8% of patients', option_d:'8 hours of treatment are required per patient', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'NNT = 1/ARR. An NNT of 8 means that for every 8 patients treated with the intervention (vs. control), 1 additional patient will benefit. Lower NNT = more effective treatment (NNT=1 means every patient benefits). NNT must be interpreted alongside risk of harm (NNH) and patient values.' },

  // --- Red Flags ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which of the following back pain presentations is a RED FLAG for malignancy?', option_a:'Morning stiffness lasting >30 minutes', option_b:'Age >50 with unexplained weight loss, pain at rest/night, and history of cancer', option_c:'Pain with prolonged sitting only', option_d:'Bilateral lower extremity weakness with coughing', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Red flags for spinal malignancy (BRRND): Bladder/bowel dysfunction (cauda equina), Rest/night pain unrelated to position, history of malignancy, unexplained weight loss, age >50 with no prior episodes, steroid use. Any combination of these with persistent back pain warrants further medical investigation before PT treatment.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient presents with acute severe low back pain, bilateral leg weakness, and reports difficulty urinating. What is the MOST appropriate action?', option_a:'Reassure the patient and begin a mobilization program', option_b:'Refer for emergency medical assessment to rule out cauda equina syndrome', option_c:'Apply heat and begin gentle stretching', option_d:'Order outpatient MRI and follow up in 2 weeks', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The presentation — bilateral leg weakness + urinary dysfunction with acute back pain — is a red flag for cauda equina syndrome (CES). CES is a surgical emergency requiring urgent MRI and possible decompression within hours to prevent permanent neurological deficit (paralysis, permanent incontinence). Immediate emergency referral is mandatory.' },

  // --- Anatomy Extra ---
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) serves as the attachment for which two muscles?', option_a:'Rectus femoris and iliacus', option_b:'Sartorius and tensor fascia latae', option_c:'Rectus femoris and pectineus', option_d:'Iliacus and psoas major', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The ASIS is the origin of sartorius (longest muscle in the body) and tensor fascia latae (TFL). The AIIS (anterior inferior iliac spine) is the origin of rectus femoris. The iliacus and psoas major originate from the iliac fossa and lumbar vertebrae respectively.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The medial collateral ligament (MCL) of the knee is MOST taut in which position?', option_a:'Full knee extension with varus stress', option_b:'Full knee extension and resists valgus stress', option_c:'90° of knee flexion only', option_d:'30° of knee flexion in a valgus position', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The superficial MCL (tibial collateral ligament) is the primary restraint to valgus stress at the knee. It is most taut (maximum tension) in full extension. At 30° flexion, both the MCL and ACL are assessed; the deep MCL and posterior capsule are more relaxed, isolating the superficial MCL for testing.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The common peroneal (fibular) nerve winds around the head of the fibula and is at risk of injury in:', option_a:'Medial knee dislocation', option_b:'Fibular head fracture or tight plaster cast at the lateral knee', option_c:'Posterior knee dislocation', option_d:'Patellar fracture', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The common peroneal nerve winds superficially around the fibular head and is vulnerable to compression or stretch injury at this point. Causes: fibular head fracture, direct blow, poorly fitted cast, leg crossing habits, intraoperative positioning. Results in foot drop + sensory loss on the dorsum of the foot.' },

  // Additional MSK questions to reach 450 total
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient with a chronic shoulder impingement is prescribed a rotator cuff exercise program. Which exercise position BEST activates the lower trapezius?', option_a:'Prone shoulder ER at 0° abduction', option_b:'Prone "Y" exercise (bilateral shoulder elevation in the plane of the spine at ~135° flexion)', option_c:'Seated shoulder press with dumbbells', option_d:'Standing side raises to 90°', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The prone Y exercise (arms elevated in the scapular-overhead plane in a Y shape) maximally activates the lower trapezius, which is critical for scapular upward rotation, posterior tilt, and retraction during overhead function. Lower trapezius inhibition is a key finding in shoulder impingement syndrome.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which muscle has dual innervation by both the femoral nerve and the obturator nerve?', option_a:'Sartorius', option_b:'Pectineus', option_c:'Vastus medialis', option_d:'Gracilis', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The pectineus is the only hip adductor that receives dual innervation: primarily from the femoral nerve (L2-L3) and sometimes from the obturator nerve (L2-L4). This makes it unique among the adductor group, which is otherwise exclusively obturator nerve innervated.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction (PTTD) is the most common cause of adult-acquired flatfoot deformity. In Stage II PTTD, which finding distinguishes it from Stage I?', option_a:'Unilateral deformity only occurs in Stage I', option_b:'Flexible flatfoot deformity with too many toes sign and inability to perform single heel rise in Stage II vs. pain only in Stage I', option_c:'Complete tendon rupture in Stage II', option_d:'Stage II is bilateral; Stage I is unilateral', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'PTTD Stages: Stage I — tendon intact, pain/swelling medially, normal alignment and heel rise. Stage II — tendon elongated/partially torn, flexible flatfoot (correctable), too-many-toes sign, failed single-leg heel rise test. Stage III — rigid flatfoot (not correctable). Stage IV — ankle valgus.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'In measuring grip strength with a handheld dynamometer, what is the standard protocol to obtain the most reliable measurement?', option_a:'Single maximum effort grip', option_b:'Three trials with the elbow at 90° flexion, wrist in neutral, and average the three readings', option_c:'Five trials with 5-minute rest between each', option_d:'Dominant hand only, single trial', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Standard grip strength protocol: seated with elbow at 90°, wrist in neutral, shoulder neutral. Three maximum voluntary contractions are performed with 60-second rest between trials. The average (or best of three) is recorded. This minimizes trial-to-trial variability and provides reliable data for comparison to normative values.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Kinesiophobia refers to:', option_a:'Fear of healthcare providers', option_b:'Fear of movement and re-injury that leads to avoidance behaviour', option_c:'Inability to perform complex coordinated movements', option_d:'Phobia of surgical procedures', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Kinesiophobia is an excessive, debilitating, and irrational fear of physical movement and activity resulting from a feeling of vulnerability to painful injury or re-injury. Measured by the Tampa Scale for Kinesiophobia (TSK). It is a strong predictor of poor outcomes in chronic pain and musculoskeletal conditions and must be addressed in rehabilitation.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which structure prevents excessive posterior tibial translation at the knee?', option_a:'Anterior cruciate ligament', option_b:'Posterior cruciate ligament', option_c:'Lateral collateral ligament', option_d:'Medial meniscus', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The PCL (posterior cruciate ligament) is the primary restraint against posterior tibial translation. It prevents the tibia from sliding posteriorly on the femur. PCL injuries typically occur from a direct anterior force to the proximal tibia (dashboard injury) or severe hyperflexion.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient presents with a positive Gaenslen\'s test. This test is used to assess:', option_a:'Lumbar disc herniation', option_b:'Sacroiliac joint dysfunction', option_c:'Hip osteoarthritis', option_d:'Piriformis syndrome', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Gaenslen\'s test: the patient lies supine at the table edge with one hip and knee flexed to the chest (fixing the lumbar spine) while the other leg hangs off the table into extension. The examiner applies pressure on the hanging leg into further extension, stressing the ipsilateral SI joint. Pain at the SI joint is a positive test.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The concept of "regional interdependence" in musculoskeletal physiotherapy means:', option_a:'Only treating the area of chief complaint', option_b:'Impairments in one region of the body may contribute to symptoms in another seemingly unrelated region', option_c:'Using multiple modalities simultaneously', option_d:'Treating multiple patients in a group setting', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Regional interdependence (Wainner et al.) proposes that dysfunction in one body region can contribute to or perpetuate symptoms in a remote region. Example: hip weakness contributing to knee pain; thoracic stiffness contributing to shoulder impingement. This justifies examination beyond the area of symptoms in PT assessment.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'During eccentric training for tendinopathy, progressive loading means:', option_a:'Maintaining constant load throughout treatment', option_b:'Systematically increasing the load as the tendon adapts over time (weeks)', option_c:'Beginning with maximum load and decreasing as pain improves', option_d:'Alternating between concentric and eccentric modes with each session', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Progressive (heavy slow resistance) loading for tendinopathy: start at a tolerable load, systematically increase resistance every 1–2 weeks as the tendon adapts. The goal is to stimulate collagen production and tendon remodeling. Pain ≤3-4/10 is acceptable during loading. Too rapid progression risks reactive flare-up.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A 22-year-old female presents with bilateral anterior knee pain, femoral anteversion, and genu valgum. Which training modification would MOST reduce dynamic valgus at the knee during squatting?', option_a:'Wider stance squatting with toes pointed in', option_b:'Hip external rotator and abductor strengthening combined with foot-ankle alignment cues', option_c:'Increasing squatting depth', option_d:'Quadriceps stretching only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Dynamic knee valgus during squatting is driven by hip IR and adduction, combined with foot pronation. Hip ER and abductor strengthening (gluteus medius, deep ER muscles) directly addresses the proximal driver. Foot/ankle orthotics may address the distal component. Movement cueing + targeted hip strengthening is most effective.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Muscle energy technique (MET) uses:', option_a:'External electrical current to produce muscle contractions', option_b:'The patient\'s own voluntary muscle contraction followed by relaxation to gain ROM', option_c:'High-velocity low-amplitude thrusts to restore joint mobility', option_d:'Sustained passive pressure on myofascial trigger points', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Muscle energy technique (MET) is an active manual therapy: the patient performs a gentle isometric contraction against the therapist\'s resistance in a specific direction for 3–10 seconds, followed by relaxation. The subsequent motion barrier is re-engaged to gain ROM. It uses post-isometric relaxation and reciprocal inhibition mechanisms.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The end-feel for a normal elbow in full extension should be:', option_a:'Springy', option_b:'Bone-to-bone (hard)', option_c:'Empty', option_d:'Tissue stretch', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'End-feels: normal bone-to-bone end-feel occurs at normal elbow extension (olecranon into fossa), wrist extension/flexion. Normal tissue stretch: shoulder IR/ER, hip ER. Springy end-feel (abnormal): indicates loose body/meniscal block. Empty end-feel (abnormal): acute bursitis, malignancy — motion stopped before normal limit due to pain.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient with a C7 radiculopathy would MOST likely demonstrate weakness in:', option_a:'Elbow flexion and supination', option_b:'Elbow extension and wrist flexion', option_c:'Shoulder abduction and elbow flexion', option_d:'Finger abduction and thumb opposition', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'C7 myotome: elbow extension (triceps), wrist flexion, and finger extension. C7 dermatome: middle finger. C7 radiculopathy presents with reduced triceps reflex, weakness of triceps and wrist flexors/finger extensors, and middle finger paresthesia. C5-C6 affects elbow flexion; C8-T1 affects hand intrinsics.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Ely test (prone knee bend) is used to assess tightness of the:', option_a:'Hamstrings', option_b:'Rectus femoris', option_c:'Iliopsoas', option_d:'Piriformis', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The Ely test: with the patient prone, the examiner passively flexes the knee. A positive test is when the ipsilateral hip involuntarily flexes off the table as the knee is bent toward the buttock, indicating rectus femoris tightness (the rectus femoris crosses both the knee and hip). The Thomas test assesses iliopsoas.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The patellofemoral joint reaction force is GREATEST at which angle of knee flexion?', option_a:'0-15° (near full extension)', option_b:'45-90° (mid-range flexion)', option_c:'90-120° of flexion', option_d:'135° (deep flexion)', correct_option_index:3, rationale:'Patellofemoral joint reaction force (PFJRF) increases with knee flexion angle because the quadriceps must generate greater force to maintain position, and the patellar contact area shifts. PFJRF at 0° ≈ 0.5 × BW; at 60° ≈ 4-5 × BW; at 90° ≈ 6-7 × BW; deepest flexion generates the highest forces. This guides exercise prescription for PFPS.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which of the following describes the close-packed position of the glenohumeral joint?', option_a:'0° abduction (arm at side)', option_b:'Full abduction and external rotation', option_c:'90° of abduction only', option_d:'Full flexion without rotation', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Close-packed position: the joint is in maximum congruence, maximum ligament tension, and minimal joint volume. For the glenohumeral joint, this is full abduction + external rotation. Loose-packed (resting) position = 55° abduction + 30° horizontal adduction (scapular plane) — used for assessment and distraction mobilizations.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Kellgren-Lawrence grading system grades the radiographic severity of:', option_a:'Rheumatoid arthritis only', option_b:'Osteoarthritis by joint space narrowing, osteophytes, sclerosis, and deformity', option_c:'Bone density loss', option_d:'Disc degeneration', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The Kellgren-Lawrence (KL) scale grades OA radiographically: Grade 0 = none; Grade 1 = doubtful narrowing, possible osteophytes; Grade 2 = definite osteophytes, possible narrowing; Grade 3 = multiple osteophytes, definite narrowing, sclerosis; Grade 4 = large osteophytes, severe narrowing, deformity. Used most commonly for the knee and hip.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A 65-year-old with Grade 4 knee OA asks about hyaluronic acid injections. Based on current evidence, the PT should advise:', option_a:'Hyaluronic acid injections have strong evidence superior to exercise', option_b:'Exercise therapy is recommended as first-line; evidence for hyaluronic acid injections is weak and inconsistent', option_c:'Injections should always precede physiotherapy', option_d:'Hyaluronic acid is a first-line treatment per all guidelines', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Current guidelines (OARSI, ACR, NICE) no longer recommend hyaluronic acid (viscosupplementation) injections for knee OA due to inconsistent evidence of clinical benefit over placebo, modest effect sizes, and cost. Exercise therapy remains the strongest evidence-based first-line treatment with the most durable effect on pain and function.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient presents with a positive compression test at the C4-C5 level. Which MOST appropriate next step should the PT take before beginning cervical manual therapy?', option_a:'Begin high-velocity manipulation immediately to relieve the nerve root', option_b:'Screen for vertebrobasilar insufficiency, myelopathy signs, and fracture risk before manual treatment', option_c:'Proceed with traction only as it is safer', option_d:'Refer directly to surgery without assessment', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Before cervical manual therapy, the PT must complete a safety screen: (1) upper motor neuron signs (myelopathy — Hoffman\'s reflex, Babinski, hyperreflexia), (2) vertebrobasilar artery testing/history, (3) fracture risk (osteoporosis, recent trauma, malignancy), (4) inflammatory arthritides (RA with atlantoaxial instability). Finding any contraindication modifies the treatment approach.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Speed\'s test is used to assess the:', option_a:'Supraspinatus integrity', option_b:'Long head of the biceps tendon and bicipital groove', option_c:'Anterior glenohumeral instability', option_d:'Acromioclavicular joint', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Speed\'s test (biceps load test): performed with the shoulder flexed to 60-90°, elbow extended, forearm supinated. Resisted shoulder flexion with pain localized to the bicipital groove is positive, suggesting biceps tendinopathy or SLAP pathology. Sensitivity ~69%, specificity ~56%.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which hip ROM is MOST restricted in the capsular pattern of hip OA?', option_a:'Flexion', option_b:'Internal rotation and flexion (IR greatest restriction)', option_c:'Abduction', option_d:'Extension', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Cyriax\'s capsular pattern for the hip joint: the greatest restriction is in internal rotation and flexion, with abduction and extension also limited, and ER relatively preserved. Internal rotation loss is often the earliest and most sensitive finding in early hip OA, preceding significant joint space narrowing on X-ray.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'What does the Functional Movement Screen (FMS) assess?', option_a:'Maximal strength of isolated muscle groups', option_b:'Movement patterns and functional movement quality across seven fundamental movement tasks', option_c:'Aerobic fitness and cardiovascular endurance', option_d:'Joint-specific ROM only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The FMS assesses seven fundamental movement patterns (deep squat, hurdle step, inline lunge, shoulder mobility, active SLR, trunk stability pushup, rotary stability) on a 0-3 scale per task (21 points max). It identifies movement dysfunction and asymmetries to guide injury prevention and corrective exercise programming.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'In chronic Achilles tendinopathy, which of the following activities should be AVOIDED in the initial loading phase?', option_a:'Eccentric heel drops on a step', option_b:'Excessive passive static stretching of the Achilles', option_c:'Isometric calf exercises', option_d:'Swimming', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Passive static stretching of the Achilles is generally contraindicated in mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy, as it compresses the tendon at the mid-portion against the calcaneus and may worsen the condition. Loading protocols (isometric, eccentric, HSR) are preferred to stimulate tendon adaptation without compressive stress.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The term "directional preference" in the McKenzie Method refers to:', option_a:'The patient\'s preferred sleeping position', option_b:'A movement direction that centralizes or abolishes symptoms and guides treatment', option_c:'The therapist\'s preferred treatment direction', option_d:'The patient\'s hand dominance', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Directional preference in the McKenzie Method is the movement direction (extension, flexion, lateral, or combined) that reliably centralizes or eliminates the patient\'s symptoms (including referred symptoms). This direction becomes the basis for repeated movement exercises and positions. Centralization is a strong positive prognostic indicator.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A 19-year-old female gymnast complains of low back pain that is worse with extension and rotation. The pain is localized to the pars interarticularis region. What condition should be suspected?', option_a:'Lumbar disc herniation', option_b:'Spondylolysis (stress fracture of the pars interarticularis)', option_c:'Sacroiliac joint dysfunction', option_d:'Lumbar facet arthropathy', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Spondylolysis (stress fracture of the pars interarticularis) is common in young athletes in extension/rotation sports (gymnastics, cricket bowling, throwing). Symptoms: unilateral low back pain worse with extension. Positive one-leg standing extension test (stork test). X-ray may be normal; CT or SPECT scan is definitive. Rest from extension activities is first-line treatment.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Oswestry Disability Index (ODI) is used to measure:', option_a:'Lumbar ROM', option_b:'Low back pain disability and functional limitation (patient-reported outcome)', option_c:'Spinal imaging severity', option_d:'Muscular strength in the lumbar region', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The ODI is a 10-item patient-reported outcome measure (PROM) assessing low back pain disability: pain intensity, personal care, lifting, walking, sitting, standing, sleeping, sex life, social life, and travelling. Score 0–20% = minimal disability; 41–60% = severe disability; 81–100% = bed-bound. MCID approximately 10–12 points.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A 30-year-old male presents with recurrent low back and buttock pain that is worse in the morning (>45 minutes), improves with movement, and has been present for 3 years. HLA-B27 positive. Which condition is MOST likely?', option_a:'Lumbar disc herniation', option_b:'Ankylosing spondylitis (axial spondyloarthritis)', option_c:'Lumbar spinal stenosis', option_d:'Non-specific low back pain', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Ankylosing spondylitis (axial SpA) features: insidious onset before age 40, duration >3 months, prolonged morning stiffness (>45 min), improvement with exercise (not rest), bilateral sacroiliac joint involvement, and positive HLA-B27 in ~90% of cases. Early diagnosis and exercise (including extension exercises) are critical to prevent spinal ankylosis.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The hip Dial test (tibial external rotation test) assesses for:', option_a:'Hip flexor tightness', option_b:'Posterolateral corner (PLC) injury of the knee', option_c:'Hip labral tear', option_d:'Sciatic nerve tension', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The Dial test assesses the posterolateral corner (PLC) of the knee. With the patient prone, external rotation of the tibia is compared bilaterally at 30° and 90° knee flexion. Increased ER at 30° only suggests isolated PLC injury; increased ER at both 30° and 90° suggests combined PLC + PCL injury.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which finding on examination indicates a complete (Grade III) medial collateral ligament (MCL) tear?', option_a:'Medial joint tenderness with no increased laxity', option_b:'Pain and medial gapping with valgus stress at 30° flexion with no firm end-feel', option_c:'Pain with valgus stress at full extension only', option_d:'Positive McMurray test with valgus loading', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'MCL grading with valgus stress at 30° flexion: Grade I = tenderness, no laxity; Grade II = pain + some laxity but firm end-feel; Grade III (complete rupture) = significant gapping (>10mm), absent end-feel (no resistance). Testing at 30° isolates the MCL; at full extension, the PCL and posterior capsule also contribute.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Hawkins-Kennedy test for shoulder impingement is performed by:', option_a:'Flexing the shoulder to 90° and applying downward pressure on the arm', option_b:'Flexing the shoulder and elbow to 90° and internally rotating the shoulder', option_c:'Abducting the shoulder to 90° with the thumb pointing down', option_d:'Extending the arm behind the back to assess subscapularis', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Hawkins-Kennedy test: the shoulder is flexed to 90°, the elbow is flexed to 90°, and the examiner forcibly internally rotates the shoulder (forcing the greater tubercle under the coracoacromial arch). Pain in the subacromial region is a positive test. Sensitivity ~72–79%, specificity ~66%.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which muscle group provides the "force couple" needed for scapular upward rotation?', option_a:'Pectoralis major and latissimus dorsi', option_b:'Upper trapezius + lower trapezius + serratus anterior', option_c:'Levator scapulae + rhomboids + middle trapezius', option_d:'Deltoid + supraspinatus only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Scapular upward rotation requires a force couple: upper trapezius (pulls the clavicle up/back), lower trapezius (pulls the scapular spine down/medial), and serratus anterior (pulls the inferior angle laterally/forward). Disruption of any component (serratus palsy, lower trap weakness) causes winging and impingement.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient with lumbar disc herniation at L4-L5 compressing the L5 nerve root would present with weakness in:', option_a:'Ankle plantar flexion (gastroc/soleus)', option_b:'Great toe and ankle dorsiflexion (EHL and TA)', option_c:'Knee extension (quadriceps)', option_d:'Hip flexion (iliopsoas)', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'L5 nerve root (exiting at L4-L5): myotome = great toe extension (EHL), foot dorsiflexion (tibialis anterior), and hip abduction. Dermatome = lateral leg and dorsum of foot. L5 radiculopathy: foot drop (particularly EHL weakness), sensory loss on dorsum of foot, no reliable reflex change (no reflex primarily innervated by L5 alone).' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis) progresses through three phases. In the correct order, these are:', option_a:'Thawing → Freezing → Frozen', option_b:'Freezing (painful) → Frozen (stiff) → Thawing (recovery)', option_c:'Acute inflammation → Repair → Remodeling', option_d:'Reactive → Disrepair → Degeneration', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Adhesive capsulitis phases: Phase 1 — Freezing (painful, up to 9 months): progressive pain and stiffness; Phase 2 — Frozen (stiff, 4–12 months): pain decreasing but stiffness maximal; Phase 3 — Thawing (12–24 months): gradual spontaneous recovery of ROM. Total duration: 1–3+ years. Some patients have persistent deficits.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The spring test (posterior-anterior pressure on spinous process) is used in the lumbar spine to assess:', option_a:'Disc herniation at the tested level', option_b:'Segmental mobility (hypomobility or hypermobility) and pain response', option_c:'Sacroiliac joint stability', option_d:'Lumbar lordosis angle', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The posterior-anterior (PA) spring test assesses lumbar segmental mobility by applying a PA force to the spinous process. The quality of the movement (stiff, normal, excessive) and the pain response (central, referred) provide information about segmental mobility impairment. Used in the McKenzie Method and Maitland assessment frameworks.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Patella alta (high-riding patella) predisposes a patient to:', option_a:'Reduced quadriceps force generation', option_b:'Increased patellofemoral instability and patellar subluxation/dislocation risk', option_c:'Increased medial compartment OA', option_d:'Achilles tendinopathy', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Patella alta means the patella is positioned higher than normal (Insall-Salvati ratio >1.2 or Caton-Deschamps >1.3). A high-riding patella has delayed engagement in the trochlear groove during knee flexion, reducing bony stability and increasing risk of lateral patellar instability, subluxation, and recurrent dislocation.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The FABER test positions the hip in:', option_a:'Flexion, adduction, and internal rotation', option_b:'Flexion, abduction, and external rotation (figure-of-four)', option_c:'Extension, abduction, and internal rotation', option_d:'Flexion, neutral, and external rotation', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'FABER = Flexion, ABduction, External Rotation — the figure-of-four position. The medial aspect of the tested knee should rest on or near the opposite knee. Inability to drop the knee to the table level (compared to the opposite side) or reproduction of pain at the SI joint, groin, or lateral hip indicates pathology in those regions.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient with a tibial plateau fracture has knee joint effusion. Which clinical sign helps differentiate a large effusion from normal?', option_a:'Pain with passive ROM', option_b:'Bulge sign (for small effusion) or ballottement/patellar tap sign (for large effusion)', option_c:'Positive McMurray test', option_d:'Decreased quadriceps strength', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Knee effusion tests: Bulge sign — milk the medial recess, tap the lateral side, observe medial bulge (small effusion). Ballottement/patellar tap — with the knee extended, fluid is milked distally and the patella is pressed: a floating patella indicates large effusion. These differentiate sizes and help diagnose intra-articular pathology.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The International Association for the Study of Pain (IASP) updated definition of pain includes:', option_a:'Pain must have a clearly identifiable tissue cause', option_b:'Pain is always acute and must resolve within 3 months', option_c:'Pain is an unpleasant sensory and emotional experience associated with or resembling that associated with actual or potential tissue damage', option_d:'Pain is only present when there is measurable tissue damage', correct_option_index:2, rationale:'The IASP 2020 definition of pain: "An unpleasant sensory and emotional experience associated with, or resembling that associated with, actual or potential tissue damage." Key update: "resembling" acknowledges that pain can exist without tissue damage (chronic pain, nociplastic pain). This biopsychosocial definition is foundational to modern PT practice.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Hypermobility Spectrum Disorder (HSD) and Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome (hEDS) share which MOST characteristic feature?', option_a:'Joint pain only, without instability', option_b:'Generalized joint hypermobility with musculoskeletal pain and recurrent joint instability/injury', option_c:'Bone fragility and fractures', option_d:'Spinal cord compression', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'HSD and hEDS involve generalized joint hypermobility (Beighton score ≥5/9), recurrent soft tissue injuries (sprains, dislocations), chronic musculoskeletal pain, and fatigue. hEDS additionally requires specific skin, family history, and systemic features. PT management focuses on joint protection, proprioceptive training, and progressive strengthening to compensate for ligamentous laxity.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'What is the primary mechanism of injury for a hamstring strain in sprinting?', option_a:'Excessive knee flexion during stance phase', option_b:'Eccentric overload of the hamstrings at high-speed running in late swing phase (terminal swing)', option_c:'Compressive loading of the hamstring during hip extension', option_d:'Tibial internal rotation during the push-off phase', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Hamstring strains in sprinting most commonly occur during terminal swing phase when the hamstrings actively decelerate the extending knee and the hip is flexed, placing the muscle under high eccentric load at long muscle length. The proximal free tendon and myotendinous junction of the long head of the biceps femoris are most commonly affected.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The Nordic hamstring exercise (NHE) program has Level I evidence for:', option_a:'Increasing hamstring concentric strength', option_b:'Reducing hamstring strain injury incidence in team sport athletes', option_c:'Treating complete hamstring tendon avulsions', option_d:'Improving hamstring flexibility only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The Nordic Hamstring Exercise (kneeling, partner-assisted eccentric deceleration) has multiple high-quality RCTs supporting 50–70% reduction in hamstring injury incidence in football/soccer players. It builds eccentric strength at long muscle lengths, the position of greatest injury risk. It is included in FIFA 11+ injury prevention programs.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which nerve root level is tested by the patellar tendon reflex (knee jerk)?', option_a:'L2-L3', option_b:'L3-L4', option_c:'L4-L5', option_d:'L5-S1', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The patellar tendon reflex (knee jerk) is mediated through L3-L4 nerve roots (primarily L4). L2-L3 primarily contribute to hip flexion and thigh adduction. The Achilles reflex is S1-S2. The biceps reflex is C5-C6; triceps reflex is C7.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The drop arm test for supraspinatus full-thickness tear is performed by:', option_a:'Having the patient attempt to resist passive shoulder abduction', option_b:'The examiner passively abducting to 90° and the patient attempting to slowly lower the arm — the arm drops suddenly', option_c:'Performing rapid overhead elevation against resistance', option_d:'Compressing the subacromial space and asking about pain', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Drop arm test: the examiner passively abducts the patient\'s arm to 90°, then the patient slowly lowers it. Inability to control the descent (arm drops suddenly) is positive for a full-thickness rotator cuff (supraspinatus) tear. Sensitivity ~27%, specificity ~88% — useful when positive due to high specificity.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Pain Catastrophizing is measured by the Pain Catastrophizing Scale (PCS). Which of the following is NOT a subscale of the PCS?', option_a:'Rumination', option_b:'Magnification', option_c:'Helplessness', option_d:'Avoidance', correct_option_index:3, rationale:'The PCS has three subscales: Rumination (can\'t stop thinking about the pain), Magnification (thinking pain will get much worse), and Helplessness (feeling nothing can reduce the pain). Avoidance is measured by the Fear-Avoidance Beliefs Questionnaire (FABQ), not the PCS. High PCS scores predict poor outcomes in musculoskeletal conditions.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The wrist extension range of motion required for normal push-up mechanics and gripping tasks is approximately:', option_a:'30°', option_b:'70–90°', option_c:'20°', option_d:'120°', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Normal wrist extension range for functional activities: approximately 70–75° is needed for push-up position (weight-bearing on extended wrists). Normal wrist extension is 60–80°. Functional ROM for most ADLs is 5° extension to 35° flexion; higher extension is needed for sports and weight-bearing tasks.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'In shoulder rehabilitation following rotator cuff repair, when is full passive ROM most commonly permitted?', option_a:'Immediately post-op (day 1)', option_b:'After 6 weeks (once tendon-to-bone healing is sufficient)', option_c:'After 12 weeks (once the repair has fully matured)', option_d:'Only after return to sport criteria are met', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Following rotator cuff repair, tendon-to-bone healing takes approximately 6–12 weeks for initial consolidation. Most protocols permit passive ROM from weeks 1–6, active-assisted ROM from weeks 6–12, and strengthening progressively thereafter. Protocol aggressiveness depends on tear size, repair quality, and tissue integrity.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'What is the most common mechanism of a posterior glenohumeral dislocation?', option_a:'FOOSH (fall on outstretched hand) with abduction and ER', option_b:'Seizure, electric shock, or direct blow to the anterior shoulder with IR and adduction force', option_c:'Anterior fall with arm behind body', option_d:'Repetitive overhead activity', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Posterior GH dislocation is uncommon (2-4% of dislocations) and commonly misdiagnosed. Mechanism: violent internal rotation force — most classically during a generalized seizure (simultaneous IR muscle contraction), electric shock, or direct blow to the anterior shoulder. The arm typically presents locked in IR and adduction.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The 10-metre walk test (10MWT) measures:', option_a:'Aerobic capacity', option_b:'Comfortable or fast gait speed (metres per second)', option_c:'Balance confidence', option_d:'Lower extremity strength', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The 10MWT measures gait speed over a 10-metre distance at comfortable and/or maximum speed. Normal community-dwelling older adult speed: ~1.2–1.4 m/s. MCID: ~0.1 m/s. Gait speed <0.8 m/s is a predictor of adverse events (hospitalization, falls, mortality) in older adults. Widely used in neurological and geriatric PT.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which test assesses sacroiliac joint provocation and is considered part of an SI joint test cluster?', option_a:'Modified Schober test', option_b:'Posterior pelvic pain provocation (PPPP / P4 test)', option_c:'FABER test in isolation', option_d:'Active straight leg raise test only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Posterior pelvic pain provocation (P4/Thigh Thrust test): supine hip flexion to 90° + adduction, femur aligned with trunk, examiner applies axial load through the femur. Pain at the SI joint is positive. Using SI test clusters (ASLR + P4 + pain with palpation of posterior SIJ + Modified Trendelenburg) improves diagnostic accuracy vs. single tests.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The clinical concept of "yellow flags" in low back pain assessment refers to:', option_a:'Physical red flags indicating serious pathology', option_b:'Psychosocial risk factors for chronicity (e.g., fear-avoidance beliefs, catastrophizing, depression, poor coping)', option_c:'Imaging findings requiring surgical referral', option_d:'Contraindications to exercise therapy', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Yellow flags are psychosocial risk factors for transition to chronic disability in acute/subacute LBP. Key yellow flags: fear-avoidance beliefs (FABQ high scores), pain catastrophizing, negative expectations, low job satisfaction, anxiety/depression, passive coping. Addressing yellow flags with education, graded activity, and psychologically informed PT improves outcomes.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which type of contraction produces the greatest muscle hypertrophy for a given load?', option_a:'Isometric', option_b:'Concentric', option_c:'Eccentric', option_d:'Isokinetic', correct_option_index:2, rationale:'Eccentric contractions produce the greatest hypertrophic stimulus for a given absolute load because: greater force per unit of muscle activation (motor unit efficiency), greater metabolic and mechanical stress, and greater muscle damage triggering satellite cell activation and protein synthesis. Eccentric overload training is used in sports performance for this reason.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient with first-episode acute low back pain (no red flags, duration <4 weeks) asks about the prognosis. The MOST evidence-based answer is:', option_a:'Prognosis is poor — most patients develop chronic pain', option_b:'The majority (>80-90%) improve significantly within 4-6 weeks with or without treatment', option_c:'X-rays must be taken to determine prognosis', option_d:'Bed rest for 2 weeks is necessary for full recovery', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Prognosis for first-episode acute non-specific LBP: ~90% of patients have significant improvement within 4–6 weeks. However, recurrence is common (~50-80% within a year), and a subset develop chronic disability. Key messages: stay active, avoid bed rest, reassure patients of favourable natural history. Early exercise and education are first-line interventions.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'What is the benefit of applying kinesiotape to the patella in patellofemoral pain syndrome?', option_a:'It permanently realigns the patella', option_b:'Short-term pain relief through skin mechanoreceptor stimulation, potentially improving quadriceps activation', option_c:'It stretches tight lateral structures permanently', option_d:'It prevents any lateral patellar movement', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Patellar taping (McConnell technique) and kinesiotape may provide short-term pain relief in PFPS, likely through altered skin afferent input from mechanoreceptors, which modifies pain processing. It does not permanently correct patellar position and should be combined with VMO strengthening, hip strengthening, and activity modification for lasting benefit.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A 25-year-old male presents with a "pop" in the hamstring during a sprinting race, immediate posterior thigh pain, and is unable to walk without significant pain. On examination, there is ecchymosis, a palpable gap at the ischial tuberosity, and significant weakness. What should be ruled out FIRST?', option_a:'Grade I hamstring strain', option_b:'Complete proximal hamstring avulsion (tendon rupture at ischial tuberosity)', option_c:'Sciatic nerve injury', option_d:'Femoral neck stress fracture', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'A felt pop + immediate severe pain + ecchymosis + palpable gap at the ischial tuberosity + significant weakness suggests a complete proximal hamstring avulsion (tendon rupture from the ischial tuberosity). This is a surgical condition; early surgical repair (<4 weeks) has superior outcomes to late repair or conservative management. Urgent MRI and orthopedic referral are needed.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which patient factor is the STRONGEST predictor of poor outcome in shoulder rotator cuff surgery?', option_a:'Patient age above 50', option_b:'Pre-operative fatty infiltration of the muscle (Goutallier grade III-IV)', option_c:'Tear size (partial vs. full thickness)', option_d:'Time since injury', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Fatty muscle infiltration (FMI), graded by the Goutallier classification on MRI/CT (Grade 0-4), is the strongest predictor of surgical failure in rotator cuff repair. Grades III-IV indicate irreversible degeneration — even after repair, the muscle cannot generate adequate force, leading to poor functional outcomes and high re-tear rates.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The primary clinical feature that distinguishes a lateral ankle sprain from a syndesmotic (high ankle) sprain is:', option_a:'The amount of swelling', option_b:'Positive squeeze test (tibfib compression) and external rotation stress test, with pain proximal to the ankle joint', option_c:'The presence of ecchymosis', option_d:'The inability to weight-bear', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Syndesmotic (high ankle) sprains involve the distal tibiofibular ligament complex. Key distinguishing tests: Squeeze test (compressing tibia and fibula at mid-calf produces distal pain) and External rotation stress test (Kleiger). Pain is proximal to the ankle and medial. Recovery is significantly longer (8–12+ weeks) than ATFL sprains (3–6 weeks).' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Blood flow restriction (BFR) training is used in early post-surgical rehabilitation because:', option_a:'It maximally loads the healing tendon or bone with heavy weights', option_b:'It allows muscle hypertrophy and strength gains with low mechanical loads (20-30% 1RM), reducing joint and healing tissue stress', option_c:'It eliminates the need for progressive loading later', option_d:'It is only appropriate for elite athletes', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'BFR training applies a cuff to restrict venous outflow (partial arterial occlusion). Exercising at 20-30% 1RM with BFR triggers metabolic and hormonal adaptations similar to heavy resistance training (hypertrophy, strength gains). This is valuable post-surgery (ACL, TKA, rotator cuff) when heavy loading is contraindicated.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The sagittal plane motion that occurs at the subtalar joint is called:', option_a:'Dorsiflexion/plantarflexion', option_b:'Supination (inversion, adduction, plantarflexion) and pronation (eversion, abduction, dorsiflexion)', option_c:'Internal/external rotation', option_d:'Abduction/adduction only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The subtalar joint (talocalcaneal) is a triplane joint. Its motion is described as: supination (combined inversion + adduction + plantarflexion) and pronation (combined eversion + abduction + dorsiflexion). It does not primarily produce pure single-plane motion. Normal subtalar inversion is ~20°; eversion ~10°. Important in foot orthotic prescription and gait analysis.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which of the following is considered a "blue flag" in the psychosocial screening framework for chronic pain?', option_a:'Fear of reinjury (psychological)', option_b:'Belief that work is harmful and compensation-related factors (occupational perceptions)', option_c:'Family reinforcement of pain behaviour (social)', option_d:'Diagnostic imaging findings', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The flags framework: Red = serious pathology; Orange = psychiatric illness; Yellow = psychological risk factors (catastrophizing, fear-avoidance); Blue = occupational perceptions (belief work will worsen pain, work dissatisfaction, poor working relationships); Black = system/contextual obstacles (litigation, compensation systems). Identifying blue flags guides vocational rehabilitation.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A patient with a positive Ober test presents with knee pain on the lateral side during running. Besides IT band stretching, the MOST evidence-based intervention is:', option_a:'Corticosteroid injection into the IT band', option_b:'Progressive hip abductor and ER strengthening, and running load management', option_c:'Complete rest for 8 weeks', option_d:'Surgical IT band release', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Iliotibial band syndrome management: reducing training load (load management), progressive hip abductor and ER strengthening (to reduce hip adduction/IR that increases IT band tension), and a graded return to running. Evidence for IT band stretching alone is weak. Surgery is a last resort after 6+ months of conservative management failure.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'A shoulder instability patient has positive apprehension test. Which rehabilitation approach is MOST evidence-based?', option_a:'Avoid all overhead activity permanently', option_b:'Progressive rotator cuff and periscapular strengthening targeting dynamic stabilizers, starting in pain-free ranges', option_c:'Immediate surgical stabilization without conservative trial', option_d:'Passive stretching of the posterior capsule only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'First-line management for anterior shoulder instability: progressive rotator cuff strengthening (subscapularis, external rotators), periscapular muscle strengthening, proprioceptive training, and gradual progression toward the apprehension position. Surgery (Bankart repair) is considered after failed conservative management or in high recurrence-risk groups (young athletes, first-time dislocation).' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The AAOS (American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons) and CAOT guidelines for carpal tunnel syndrome recommend as first-line treatment:', option_a:'Immediate surgical carpal tunnel release', option_b:'Neutral wrist splinting, activity modification, and nerve/tendon gliding exercises', option_c:'Corticosteroid injection as first-line only', option_d:'Rest alone for 4 weeks', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'For mild-to-moderate CTS, first-line conservative treatment: neutral wrist splinting (especially at night), activity/ergonomic modification, and nerve/tendon gliding exercises. Corticosteroid injection may be used for moderate symptoms. Surgical CTR (carpal tunnel release) is recommended for severe or conservative-treatment-resistant CTS. PT is effective for mild-moderate CTS.' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'The long thoracic nerve (C5-C7) innervates the serratus anterior. Injury results in:', option_a:'Shoulder internal rotation weakness', option_b:'Medial (classic) winging of the scapula during forward flexion', option_c:'Inability to abduct the shoulder beyond 30°', option_d:'Loss of shoulder flexion strength only', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'Long thoracic nerve palsy → serratus anterior paralysis → medial scapular winging (inferior angle wings away from the thorax during forward arm elevation). Classic winging is medial and seen during forward flexion/pushing. Spinal accessory nerve palsy causes trapezius weakness and lateral winging (superior angle wings medially).' },
  { domain:'Musculoskeletal', template_id:1, question_text:'Which of the following assessment tools measures patient-reported shoulder function and is validated for rotator cuff conditions?', option_a:'Roland Morris Disability Questionnaire', option_b:'DASH (Disabilities of the Arm, Shoulder, and Hand) questionnaire', option_c:'Oswestry Disability Index', option_d:'Harris Hip Score', correct_option_index:1, rationale:'The DASH (and its short form QuickDASH) is a validated PROM measuring upper extremity disability and function. It is widely used for shoulder, elbow, and hand conditions. MCID for shoulder = ~10–15 points. Condition-specific alternatives include the PENN Shoulder Score, ASES, and WORC (specifically for rotator cuff conditions).' },
];
